     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't solve either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 5100 mg a day is twice the maximum approved daily dose of metformin, and confirming it doesn't make it safe for the patient.\n- **It still needs a prescriber.** A medication order has to come from the patient's treating clinician. That's especially true for one this far outside the normal range, which would need clear clinical reasoning documented by a prescriber, and probably a pharmacist's review too.\n\n**Here's what I'd suggest:**\n1. Contact the patient's attending physician or the covering prescriber on the medicine service and ask them to confirm the intended dose.\n2. If a different dose was intended, such as 850 mg two or three times a day or 1700 mg a day in total, the prescriber can give me the corrected order and I'll enter it.\n3. If you think this order came through as a mistake somewhere along the way, it's worth flagging to pharmacy or patient safety.\n\nI'm glad to help enter a corrected order as soon as a prescriber provides one."
      }
     ],
